        ":question/id": "d82a128a-970f-55e8-b4ad-4c3c16a14935",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-105075",
        ":question/problem": "![](images/34/3411e11060b06a11c82ad677f50731faa31c6bc2e83ccb0d0d10931cf3d76592.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true everywhere in the domain of $F?$\n\n- $\\text{div}\\,F=0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F>0$\n- $\\text{div}\\,F<0$\n- $F$ is solenoidal",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Recall that $\\text{div}\\,F=∇\\cdot F$ denotes the divergence of the vector field $F.$\n\nIntuitively, the divergence of $F$ at a point $P$ measures how much $F$ acts like a source or a sink at $P:$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F>0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like a source at $P.$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F<0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like a sink at $P.$\n\n- If $∇\\cdot F=0$ at $P,$ then $F$ acts like neither a source nor a sink at $P.$\n\n![](images/6c/6c747149618d4c5ef35e727af15cd001365bd9c2d5e064689baf24c6ee23cff9.png)\n\nIf $∇\\cdot F=0$ everywhere in the domain of $F,$ we say that $F$ is solenoidal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement III is true, whereas statements I and II are false. Notice that the lengths of the vectors decrease as we move in the $y$-direction. Therefore, the vector field $F$ acts like a sink at every point.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since $∇\\cdot F\\ne 0,$ the vector field $F$ is not solenoidal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
